                            Strategic FALCON
                         
                           Version 1.21 Update
         
         
       Version 1.21 is mainly a bug fix for version 1.2, but there 
       are a couple of added features to make it worth the time to 
       download. 

                                      
       Select Formations:
         
         Finally! You can now set all your flights' formations from
         inside StratFalc--so you're no longer limited to "Wedge" for 
         all StratFalc flights. A "Form" (Formation) button has 
         been added to the tactical flight submodes' control panels. 
         When you press this button, a dialog box pops up that allows 
         you to choose the formation for your flights. You'll also 
         see a graphic representation of the formation, which 
         (hopefully) will make it a easier to decide which one to 
         use.
                                      
         The separation distance of the aircraft in your F-16 flight 
         is set within Falcon using the wingman commands. The normal 
         separation (as detailed in the OFT manual) is about 680 
         feet. You can close this up to 60 feet with <Shift-F> or 
         open it to about 7600 feet with <Shift-G>. Since this is set 
         actively in Falcon, the "Separation" box in the Form dialog 
         is disabled for F-16 flights.
         
         For StratFalc flights (that is, Allied or Enemy), the
         separation box is active. Falcon normally sets the 
         separation of these aircraft to 90 feet, which is a little 
         too close for comfort. They're more effective when they're 
         spread out a some but still close enough together to perform 
         maneuvers. 500 to 1000 feet is good.
         
         And for those who want to know that little bit extra: the 
         "Change Scale" command in Falcon directly affects aircraft 
         spread. If you jump from 1X to 2X, for instance, your 
         wingman will spread just as if you had issued the "Kick 
         Out" command.
         
         Note: On some systems, the use of the bitmaps in the 
         formation dialog causes a very annoying flicker.  If 
         yours is one of them, you can turn off the bitmaps by 
         using the "NOPIC" switch ("STRAT /NOPIC") when you start 
         StratFalc.
 
         
       Start Enemy Flights from Airbases: 
                   
         There's a new box in town. If you hit the "More..." button 
         under Other/Config, you'll see a check box labeled "Move 
         Enemy Flights to Airbases." If you check this box, StratFalc
         will examine the enemy flights in the current mission. If it 
         finds any enemy airbases close enough to a red flight's 
         starting point, it will move that starting point to one of 
         those bases. How close is "close enough"? Well, that depends 
         on the mission distances involved, but StratFalc will 
         *never* take the enemy out of play!
         
         This adds a couple new elements to Falcon. One, it serves 
         our great god "realism": having enemy flights just 
         conveniently orbit the target all the time is a bit much. 
         Now, if you lock out enemy views, you can't always predict 
         what you'll be up against, or where the threat will 
         originate.
         
         Two, you must now study your target with an eye for the 
         location of the surrounding air bases, because the enemy 
         response will indeed be coming from one of those bases (and 
         it isn't always the closest one, either!).
         
         A few mission types will not be altered, most notably 
         scramble and defensive screen missions. The positioning of 
         the enemy aircraft is more or less critical here and the 
         aircraft in these missions usually radiate from an enemy 
         base anyway.
         
         Checking this box does more than just move the enemy 
         flight, though.  It's designed to allow you to lock out 
         enemy views and not worry about the enemy flights' 
         armaments anymore.  In case you haven't noticed, Falcon 
         often places the wrong armaments on the enemy and allied 
         aircraft when you're assigned a bomber or strike escort.  
         If this box is enabled, StratFalc will examine the armament 
         of those flights and automatically correct it.  By doing 
         this, StratFalc makes sure you never have to peek at the 
         enemy planes at all. 

         Don't forget to hit "Save Cfg" if you want this option 
         to be permanent.     

                        
       Move the Carrier:

         In Sim Mode, you now have the ability to move the carrier. A 
         "Move Carrier" button has been added to the "Bases" 
         submode (it will only be displayed when the carrier is 
         the current base). If you press this button, a dialog box 
         will pop up with radio buttons, labeled "Position Alpha" 
         through "Position Echo." 
         
         Each theater (except Pakistan) has five pre-arranged carrier 
         launch and recovery coordinates. Press the radio buttons to 
         see these locations. Press "OK" to move the carrier to the 
         currently displayed coordinates. If there are any active 
         flights off the carrier for the current mission, you'll be 
         asked if you want them moved to the new coordinates.
         
         Br warned, however, that you can only move the carrier 
         three times during a campaign!  After that, the "Move 
         Carrier" button won't appear, so plan wisely!
                                
                     
       Alter Pilot Skills:
       
         Another new configuration option is the ability to 
         automatically set a range for allied and enemy pilot 
         skills.  This gives you finer control over the difficulty 
         levels you go up against in Falcon. StratFalc will generate 
         a random value within the range you select and set the 
         pilots' skill levels accordingly.
         
         StratFalc generates three numbers for each pilot: one for 
         flying skill, another for ACM ability, and a third one 
         or bombing ability.  If you want to force a specific 
         number, just set that number as both the min and the max.  
         For instance, if you always want your allied pilots to be
         prefect, put the number "100" in both of the "Alter Allied 
         Pilot Skills" boxes.
                       
       
       Updated Descriptions:
         
         StratFalc will now do a little string manipulation to your 
         mission description file whenever you change aircraft 
         types.  If you want this left alone, check the 
         "Do Not Alter Mission Description" block in the "More 
         Config" dialog and press the "Save Cfg" button.
                                      

       Campaign Day and Time: 
         
         Several times I've found myself looking in the bottom left 
         corner of the screen for the current mission day and time, 
         in the same place it's always displayed in Falcon.  I got
         sick of not seeing it, so I added this option to the 
         Other/Map screen. Don't forget to hit the "Set" button if 
         you want it there permanently.
                                                            
                                                             
       End-of-Campaign Hangs:
         
         It turns out that near the end of a campaign, Falcon 
         exhibits some very strange behavior.  If the enemy has 
         lost *all* of the defeat nodes (his "victory nodes") by
         2300 on any given day, the sim hangs.  
         
         StratFalc now takes an active role in getting around this.  
         Each time it is run, it will examine the occupation of the 
         defeat nodes.  As long as the enemy owns at least one, 
         it'll do nothing.  But if it sees that you've taken over all 
         of the defeat nodes, it springs into action.  What it does
         is simply change one of these nodes to a site that the enemy
         still holds (and that isn't already a defeat node). If it 
         can't find any that fit this description, it may have to 
         surrender one of your sites to the enemy. This is a *very* 
         rare occurrence (actually, I doubt you'll ever see it 
         happen). If StratFalc does have to do this, it will tell you 
         so.
         
         If you want to avoid this entirely, just make sure that one 
         of the enemy defeat nodes is tucked away in the far reaches 
         of the theater, where it'll be the last thing you take.
         
         Please understand that moving these defeat nodes does not 
         alter your requirements for victory at all--it just alters 
         the *enemy's* requirements for victory. If this routine 
         kicks in at all, you're very close to winning the campaign 
         anyway--there's no way he's about to win if you have all 
         the defeat nodes!
         
  
       <TAB> Key Support in Dialog Boxes: 
         
         In order to be a little more user-friendly, you'll see that 
         several of the dialog boxes in StratFalc now have "default"
         buttons.  The default button will have an extra gray border 
         around it.  If you hit the <Enter> key, the default button 
         will be activated.  The <TAB> key cycles though the buttons. 

                               
       STRTSPRT.COM/PADLOCK.COM conflicts:
         
         A couple reports have come in that STRTSPRT and PADLOCK
         produce a conflict and hang some systems. This can be fixed 
         by loading PADLOCK.COM before STRTSPRT.COM. It's easiest to 
         do this in your autoexec.bat.  If for some reason you do 
         not want to do this, use the <F6> USER.BAT technique
         described in the 111_READ.ME file to create a batch file 
         that will load the TSRs (Padlock then Strtsprt), run 
         Falcon, then (if required) unload the TSRs in reverse order.
         
         (In case you don't know what it is, PADLOCK is a great
         TSR by Ken Richardson that places visual cues about the 
         state of your aircraft into the padlock and forward-up views 
         in Falcon.) 

                   
       Miscellaneous Changes and Notes:
         
         * A new routine has been added to make sure your data files
           are valid--if your *.MIS file appears to have been generated 
           by a previous Falcon mission you'll get the following 
           prompt:         
  
              "Mission file is old!  Run Falcon and get into
                 the cockpit.  Do you wish to do so now?"
           
           If you answer yes, StratFalc will start Falcon so you can
           take care of the problem immediately. Just go through the
           campaign screens and get into the cockpit, then quit to DOS.
           After you do so you'll be automatically returned to
           StratFalc. (Because of the complications presented by
           pregenerated missions in the Kuriles, this safety feature
           does not work reliably there.)

         * If you're using Sim Mode and you haven't looked at your
           Brigades yet for the current mission, the phrase "-Check
           Brigades-" will appear under the scale of miles (if you
           have the scale turned on).  The words will vanish after
           you've entered the Strat/Sim/Ground submode.  I put this
           here because I was always forgetting to move my brigades.

         * The "Open" dialog has been altered so that you can now see
           the numbers of the squadrons you're using.  This is handy
           if you want to back up data files or if you have to run
           "RESTRCAM" for any reason.

         * You'll see that the planes in the "Select Aircraft..."
           dialog have been alphabetized to make it easier on you.
           
         * It's never really been mentioned:  you need at least 
           580K free conventional memory to run StratFalc dependably. 
           This shouldn't be much of a problem since you're obviously 
           set up for Falcon, and it needs 602K!
           
         * When you add an F-16 flight, SF will now ask you if you 
           want to copy the waypoints of the first F-16 flight.  This 
           should make it easier to set up accompanying flights.
           
         * One thing that I don't think I made clear in the 1.2 
           version docs: if you are going to Red Flag, and if you've 
           just used StratFalc to fly a non-F16 aircraft in a 
           campaign, then you should copy UNHACK.EXE from your \STRAT 
           directory into the Falcon directory and run it. This will
           put Falcon back to normal so you can load weapons properly 
           in Red Flag.
           
         * Here's a special note about flying the swing-wing 
           aircraft: these puppies initially use more elbow room than
           fixed-wing types. If you are flying an F-111, F-14, or 
           Tornado, you'll discover when you first get into the 
           cockpit that your wingman is not on your wing; instead, 
           he's about three miles south of your current position, at 
           650 feet (regardless of the scale you're using). This can 
           cause real problems in mountainous areas, where your 
           wingman can start out in the side of a mountain. In these 
           cases, if the swing-wing type is the only aircraft for the 
           job, you'll have to put yourself into a flight of one and 
           add an additional flight for the other necessary aircraft.
           
         * Here's another special note which only applies to Bosnia: 
           You'll get some strange results in debrief if you use
           Strategic Falcon to change plane types here--things like
           "Empty18" and "Empty21"--because StratFalc is allowing
           you to have aircraft that Falcon wouldn't normally place 
           in that theater.  (This could be fixed by replacing the 
           strings in BOSNIA.MSC with the proper plane names, if
           you're interested in doing so.)  In any case, it won't
           hurt anything.

         
       Finally, the bug fixes and their ilk:
         
         There weren't really that many! The main one was a mistake 
         I made with the FTP interface--if you hit "Save" before 
         hitting "Exit," you wouldn't be prompted for the flight 
         model! There were a few more tweaks to the FTP interface. 
         Also, momentum adjustments have been improved, and a few 
         "t"s have been dotted and "i"s crossed.
         
      
       That's it. Hope you enjoy the new features!
         
